# 12. Leave Without Slamming the Door

There are two ways to fail at leaving home, and most men have done one of them.

The first is the one you're picturing. Eighteen or twenty-two or thirty, a fight in the kitchen, a bag in the truck, and a door slammed hard enough to rattle the frame. Then three states away and a phone call at Christmas if the man is feeling generous. Some of you did it with more noise than that and some of you did it with less, but the shape was the same. You left angry, and the anger came with you, and it's still riding in the truck.

The second way is quieter, and I see it more often. The man never left at all. He moved out, maybe. He may be forty-four with a mortgage. But the center of gravity in his life is still his parents' house. He can't make a decision without hearing his father's voice on it. His wife has spent fifteen years competing with his mother and losing. He calls his dad before he calls his pastor, and his dad still tells him what to do, and he still does it. He didn't slam the door. He never walked through it. God's design for a man is neither one, and it's in the second chapter of the Bible, before there was a single broken family to complicate it.

:::margin
Genesis 2:24 (NIV)
"That is why a man leaves his father and mother and is united to his wife, and they become one flesh."
:::

A man leaves, the text says, and that's God talking, not a rebellious teenager, and leaving is the design. Notice that it comes before "is united to his wife," which means leaving isn't only a marriage thing. A single man has to leave too. A man's life is supposed to have its own center, under God, and that center is not his parents' kitchen. But the same God who said "leave" also said this, and put it in the ten commandments where nobody could miss it.

:::margin
Exodus 20:12 (NIV)
"Honor your father and your mother, so that you may live long in the land the LORD your God is giving you."
:::

Leave, and honor, both at once. A man who slams the door has left and failed to honor. A man who never leaves has honored, after a fashion, and failed to leave. The man God is after does both together, which is harder than either one alone, and it's the subject of this chapter.

## Why you have to leave

Let me speak first to the man who never walked through the door, because he's the one who doesn't think this chapter is for him.

If you're married and your mother's opinion still outranks your wife's in your own house, you haven't left. If your father can make you feel twelve years old with one comment, and you arrange your decisions to avoid that comment, you haven't left. If you're single and thirty-five and you've never made a major decision without running it past your parents first, you haven't left.

I'm not telling you to stop loving them, and I'm not telling you to stop listening to them; a wise man takes counsel from his elders his whole life. I'm saying that the man God made you to be has to stand on his own ground, answerable to God, and that ground can't be a room in your parents' house. Your wife needs to know that she's first. Your children need to see a father who's actually the father, not a grown boy relaying instructions from the grandparents. And you need it. A man who never leaves never becomes the corner post of his own place. He stays a line post in somebody else's fence for the rest of his life.

Leaving well looks like this. You make the decision and then tell them, instead of asking them and then deciding. You say "I love you, and we're going to do it this way," and you don't argue the point. You protect your wife from your mother's criticism even when your mother happens to be right. You stop needing your father's approval before you act, which is the work of the last two chapters. And you do all of it with a soft voice and a steady spine, because leaving well isn't a fight. It's a man walking out of a room he has outgrown, gently, and closing the door behind him without a sound.

## Why you can't slam it

Now the other man, the one who left hard. You may have had every reason. Some of you left houses that no boy should have had to live in, and I'm not going to tell you that you should have stayed, and I'm not going to tell you to go back to a man who's still dangerous. But I am going to tell you what a slammed door does, because I've watched it in too many men to stay quiet about it.

A slammed door doesn't stay behind you. It comes with you. The man who cut off his father in fury has his father in every room of his life, because fury is a kind of attention, and the thing you're furious at is the thing your life is organized around. He isn't free of his dad. He's more bound to him than the son who calls every Sunday. He simply can't see it, because the binding is made of anger instead of affection.

And the slammed door does one other thing. It teaches your children how to leave you.

Kids watch how their father treats his father. That's the manual they're given. If they see contempt, they learn that contempt is what grown sons feel for old men, and they file that away for the day when you're the old man. If they see a father who honors a man who didn't deserve it, they learn something about honor that no sermon could teach them.

So you can't slam it. Not because your father earned better; maybe he didn't. Because you're a man, and men don't slam doors, and because the Father who did not leave you is watching how you handle the father who did.

## What honor means when he wasn't honorable

This is where men get stuck, so let me be concrete. Honor doesn't mean pretending. You don't have to say your father was good if he wasn't; Scripture never asks you to lie. Honor is about how you treat the office, not how you rate the performance. A soldier salutes the rank even when he can't respect the man wearing it, and he isn't being a hypocrite. He's being a soldier.

Honoring a hard father looks like telling the truth about him without mocking him, because there's a difference between "my father was a hard man and he hurt us" and the version you'd tell at a bar to get a laugh. It looks like speaking about him carefully in front of your children, who don't need to carry your contempt for their grandfather, and if you hand it to them, that's your sin and not his. It looks like providing for him if he needs it in old age, even if he never provided for you; Jesus had hard words for men who found religious reasons to skip that. It looks like not wishing him harm and not rejoicing when harm comes. And it looks like praying for him, honestly, even if the prayer is only "Father, I don't know how to pray for this man, but You know him."

And it looks like forgiving him. That's the heaviest one, so let me be careful about what it means.

## Forgiveness

Forgiveness is not saying it didn't matter. It mattered, and God knows exactly how much.

Forgiveness is not trust. You can forgive a man and still not leave your children alone with him. Trust is earned and rebuilt over time; forgiveness is given.

Forgiveness is not necessarily a relationship. Some of you will forgive fathers you will never sit at a table with again, because the table isn't safe, and that isn't a failure of forgiveness.

Here's what forgiveness is. It's a man deciding that the debt his father owes him is going to be settled by God and not by him. He stops collecting, and he stops waiting for the apology as the condition of his own peace. He hands the whole account over to the only Judge who has all the facts and lets it leave his hands.

:::margin
Romans 12:19 (NIV)
"Do not take revenge, my dear friends, but leave room for God's wrath, for it is written: 'It is mine to avenge; I will repay,' says the Lord."
:::

Most men read "leave room for God's wrath" as a threat against their enemies. Read it as a relief. You don't have to be the one who makes him pay. That was never your job, and you can set it down. Joseph's brothers threw him in a pit and sold him and let their father believe he was dead. Years later, when he had every power to make them pay for it, he said something that's the clearest picture of forgiveness I know.

:::margin
Genesis 50:20 (NIV)
"You intended to harm me, but God intended it for good to accomplish what is now being done, the saving of many lives."
:::

He didn't say they hadn't intended harm. He named it: you intended to harm me. Then he said what God had done with it anyway. That's the shape of the thing. Full honesty about the wrong, full trust that God was working in it regardless, and no bill presented.

## The conversation

If your father is alive and it's safe, there may be a conversation to have. Not everyone needs to have it; some fathers can't hear it, and some conversations only reopen a wound God is already closing. But for a lot of men it's the door they've been standing outside of for years, and I want to give you a way through it.

You wrote a letter in chapter 8, the one you weren't going to send. Read it again, because it's your preparation, though it isn't your script. The conversation itself is shorter than the letter, and it has four parts.

First, what you needed and didn't get. Say it once, without a list of examples. "Dad, I needed to hear you say you were proud of me, and I never did." One sentence. Don't pile on, because he'll hear one sentence and he won't hear ten.

Second, what you're grateful for, and you'll need to find something, because even a hard father usually gave you something: a work ethic, a roof, a hunting trip, a sentence at the right moment. Name it, and know that this isn't flattery; it's honor.

Third, what you're going to do. "I'm going to raise my kids differently, and I'm not going to carry this against you anymore." Say it as a statement and not a threat. You're telling him what's yours.

Fourth, the door, which sounds like this: "I'd like to know you as a man, if you're willing." And then leave it there. Don't demand that he change. Don't wait for an apology as the price of the door staying open. You've said your piece like a man, and how he answers is his weight to carry, not yours.

Cole did this with his father, the one who'd never said a kind word in thirty-three years. He drove out to the old man's place on a Tuesday and said the four things sitting on the tailgate. His father didn't apologize, and he didn't say he was proud. He said, "Well," and looked at the barn for a while, and then he said, "You want to look at that pump with me?" Cole said yes. He told me later that was the closest thing to an embrace his father had ever offered, and he took it, because he'd stopped needing more than the man could give.

If your father is dead, or too dangerous to approach, have the conversation anyway. Say the four things to God, out loud, and say them to a brother, and write them down. The words need to leave you even if they can't reach him.

## About your mother

Genesis 2:24 says a man leaves his father and mother, and I've been talking mostly about fathers, so let me turn.

Some of you were raised by your mother alone, and she was the whole family, and she was strong, and you love her more than anyone on earth, and that's good. Leave her anyway, because she raised you to be a man, and a man has his own house. If you're married, your wife has to be first, and your mother needs to hear you say so, kindly and clearly, the first time there's a conflict rather than the tenth. If you're single, you still need a life whose center isn't your mother's kitchen table, or you'll never be ready for a wife when she comes, and your mother, if she's wise, knows that better than you do.

If you're divorced, watch this one closely. There's a pull in the year after a divorce to go home emotionally, to let your mother become your confidante and your children's second mother and your reason not to build a life of your own. She loves you and she'll let you, and you mustn't. Honor her, lean on her some, and stay a man with his own ground.

## To the older man whose son slammed the door

Some of you are reading this from the other side, as the father. Your son left hard, or drifted, or calls on Christmas, and you don't know what you did, or you do.

Here is what I'd say: leave the door unlocked, because that's the whole strategy. You can't make him come back and you shouldn't try. But you can be the father in Jesus's story, who went out to both his sons, the one who ran off and the one who stayed home bitter.

:::margin
Luke 15:28 (NIV)
"The older brother became angry and refused to go in. So his father went out and pleaded with him."
:::

His father went out. Twice in that story the father is the one who moves, so go first. Make the call even if he doesn't pick up. Send the text on his birthday with nothing in it but love. Don't keep score of who called last, and don't demand an apology as the price of the door. And if he comes, don't relitigate anything. Just set another plate.

And if there's something you did, say so. Plainly, once, without excuses. "I wasn't the father you needed. I'm sorry." A grown son who hears that from his father, even at fifty, even at seventy, will feel something shift that he didn't know could move. Roy did this with his daughter, who'd been distant for a decade after his years on the road. He said the sentence in her driveway. She didn't answer for a long time. Then she asked if he wanted to come in for supper.

## Leaving at every age

Men leave home more than once, and they fail at it in the same two ways at every age.

A young man of twenty is leaving for the first time, and he's tempted toward one door or the other: the slammed one, where he cuts his parents off in the name of freedom, or the one he never walks through, where he calls his mother before every decision and lets his father's opinion run his life from two hundred miles away. The takeaway for him is that leaving well at twenty looks like making his own decisions and telling his parents afterward with love, and that this is practice for a marriage.

A young father is leaving a second time, because the arrival of a child pulls grandparents back into the center of a house with opinions about everything. Tomás's mother and his wife disagreed about how the baby should sleep, and Tomás had to say, kindly and in front of his mother, that they'd be doing it his wife's way. The takeaway for him is that a man protects his wife from his mother's criticism even when his mother is right.

A man of forty-five is often leaving for the third time, because his father is aging and the old dynamics come back. He finds himself twelve years old in the truck after every visit. The takeaway for him is the four-part conversation, which is not too late at forty-five and may be the most important conversation of his decade.

An older man is on the other side of the door, watching a son who left hard or drifted, and the takeaway for him is the father in Jesus's story, who went out to both sons. Leave the door unlocked, make the call that doesn't get answered, and say the sentence if there's a sentence to say. Roy's supper with his daughter came after ten years of an unlocked door.

## Walk out, and don't slam

You have to leave. That's God's design, and you can't be a man without it. Your life has to have its own center, under Him, and it can't be in your parents' house.

And you have to honor. That's God's command, and you can't be a man without that either. Whatever kind of chair sat at the end of your table, the man in it was your father, and how you treat him now is a mark that shows on you.

So walk out, gently, with a steady spine, closing the door behind you without a sound and leaving it unlocked. Then go build a house of your own, and make sure the door on it is the kind a son can come home through.

## Before you turn the page

Honoring a father who wasn't honorable is one of the hardest things this book asks, and I don't want you to read it as a demand that you pretend. God never asks a man to lie about his father. He asks him to hand the debt over, and to salute the office, and to walk out gently, and He asks it because He knows that a slammed door rides in the truck for the rest of a man's life. May the Father who did not leave you give you the steadiness to leave without slamming, and the grace to leave the door unlocked behind you, and may your children learn from watching you how to leave you well someday.


## YOUR TURN

---

**Name it.** Because both ways of failing at leaving hide from the man who's doing them: say which one is yours, the slammed door or the door you never walked through, and say what it's cost your wife or your kids or your own life.

**Do this week.** Because leaving and honoring are both done in specific acts: if you never left, make one decision this week without running it past your parents first and tell them afterward with love; if you slammed the door, pray for your father by name every day this week, and if he's alive and safe, pick a date for the four-part conversation.

**Tell a brother.** Because a conversation with a date on it that another man knows about actually happens: tell him which father you have and which door you've been standing at, and if the conversation is coming, tell him when.

**Carry this.** Honor the office, tell the truth about the man, hand the debt to God, and leave the door unlocked. Leave. Don't slam.
